WEBVTT

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.620
When Galileo first gazed through his telescope 400 years ago,

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.090
his discoveries changed the way mankind saw his place in the universe.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.180
For the first time, he was able to observe craters and mountains on the moon.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:29.280
He discovered four of Jupiter's moons and proved they orbited the giant planet.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.850
He was also the first person to view the rings of Saturn.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.280
Today, while optical telescopes are larger and more powerful,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.980
they are still constructed using the same principles as in Galileo's time.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.260
But as we peer deeper into the universe,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.510
interstellar gas and dust interferes with visible light,

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:53.610
blocking our view into the past.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.460
One solution is to change how we look at the stars.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:00:59.960
Instead of using...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.230
visible light, astronomers can use one type of invisible light, infrared radiation, to

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:12.610
study the heavens. With its longer wavelengths, infrared radiation allows us to gaze deeper

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:20.180
into the universe and farther into the past. Soon, astronomers will be able to use the

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:25.920
world's largest and most powerful airborne infrared observatory to make studies bearing

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.000
on the questions, how did the sun

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.890
and earth form and what

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.300
conditions led life to start on Earth.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.890
The Stratospheric Observatory for Infrared Astronomy, or SOFIA, is a specially modified

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:59.760
Boeing 747SP aircraft housing an infrared telescope assembly that weighs 17 tons.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.600
and has an eight-and-a-half-foot diameter mirror.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:08.540
The Flying Observatory is designed to detect the formation of stars in our galaxy,

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.080
determine the chemical composition of the interstellar medium,

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:17.530
and peer through the dust that hides the black hole at the center of the Milky Way.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.840
SOFIA will fly between 41 and 45,000 feet above sea level,

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.140
which is above 99% of the water vapor in our atmosphere.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:29.940
The water in the atmosphere.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.400
absorbs infrared so we got to get above that water vapor and that's why we put

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.600
the infrared telescope into the back of the 747 so that we get up into the

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.080
stratosphere where there is no absorption by the water vapor.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.940
However mounting a 17-ton telescope assembly in the tail of an airplane

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:56.770
presented an engineering challenge. A 16-foot high hole was cut into the rear

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.910
fuselage of the aircraft and doors were installed to

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.320
protect the mirror when not imaging the universe.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:11.320
The modification itself is rather extensive in that there was a very large hole cut in

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.320
the back of the airplane.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.920
The hole that was cut was about four by five meters.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.280
It's the largest hole that I'm aware of that exists in any 747.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.730
This made a bump on the airplane where the doors cover this hole and that we have to verify

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.960
that that bump hasn't caused additional drag that would...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.880
preclude us from being able to meet our time requirement at altitude and also to verify

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.800
that the stability and control characteristics of the airplane haven't been influenced or

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.280
affected by this modification.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:50.380
The telescope was built by NASA's partner DLR, the German Aerospace Center. Astronomers

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:55.820
hope the size, sensitivity and versatility of the observatory will put SOFIA in the

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.520
rank of one of NASA's great observatories.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.410
G, capital O, Great Observatories, is a special designation that NASA has reserved to four

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:11.490
separate observatories. The Hubble, which is a visible telescope, Chandra, which is an

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:17.160
X-ray telescope, the Compton, which is a gamma-ray telescope, and Spitzer, which is an infrared

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:23.460
telescope. SOFIA is certainly a major NASA observatory in roughly the same category of

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.760
those that is capable of doing great observatory class science.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.320
Mounting a telescope in the back of an airplane offers several advantages.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.880
One is the ability to quickly position SOFIA anywhere in the world to observe the night sky.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.920
The second major advantage is that SOFIA comes home after every mission.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:55.470
Scientists and technicians will have the chance to add or replace instruments in order to adapt to changing astronomical events and new technologies.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.640
Technicians will also be able to repair any necessary parts.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.190
and replenish supplies.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.520
Because of this flexibility,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:07.070
the projected lifespan of SOFIA is 20 years,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.100
while space-based observatories

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.560
typically last between five and 10 years.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.080
SOFIA is currently undergoing flight testing

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.040
at NASA's Dryden Aircraft Operations Facility

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.480
in Palmdale, California.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.800
NASA Dryden is responsible

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.880
for the aircraft's technical operations,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.960
while the science program is overseen by scientists at NASA.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.100
NASA's Ames Research Center at Moffett Field, California.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:38.060
Flight testing is currently scheduled to continue this year,

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.540
with science missions beginning in the spring of 2010.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.980
SOFIA will be a key component of NASA's astrophysics program,

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.940
answering fundamental questions about the universe.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:53.710
The capabilities of the aircraft and the telescope

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.660
offers researchers a quantum leap forward

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.130
as they explore the hidden universe.
